{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And they shall confess their iniquity, etc.</b> The earlier authorities did not enlighten our eyes regarding these verses, for all of them are difficult to hear. First — why did the verse obligate them to confess the iniquity of their fathers? Is it not the case that by confessing their own iniquity I do not call them ones who hold the deeds of their fathers in their hands (Berakhot 7a)? Second — its saying ״אַף אֲנִי אֵלֵךְ עִמָּם״ etc.: what kind of punishment is this after the confession? Third — its saying ״וְהֵבֵאתִי אוֹתָם״ etc. — this punishment has already been stated above. Fourth — its saying <b>אוֹ אָז יִכָּנַע</b> — why this expression of uncertainty? And the Ramban, z״l, explained that the uncertainty concerns the redemption — whether it will come through contrition or only after they have accepted their punishment — and this is a far-fetched interpretation. And likewise what our master Rashi, z״l, explained is far-fetched to the seeing eye and the understanding heart." "<b>And it appears</b> to explain the verses in this manner: for since the error of habitual sinners comes to them from a misleading source — in that they see what is before them, how they practiced iniquity year after year and their way succeeded and their tranquility was beyond measure; even though their sin was heavier than this, they would not fear to do evil nor consent to accept reproof, for their confidence in what is visible grows greater than their acknowledgment of the rebuke from the Torah's promises. And this matter consumed the faith of the people of Israel concerning the punishment of the wicked and the reward of the righteous. And likewise, behold, the people of Israel — HaShem did not bring reproof upon them until the earlier iniquities multiplied and rose to the highest point, and their tranquility and prosperity was very great and immense, as is understood from the words of the prophets. Therefore, when HaShem acted and executed upon them the retribution for their iniquities, they did not believe that it was paid to them for practicing evil, and they would seek proof from what happened to the earlier generations. Therefore HaShem said: at the time of their return, when they come to confess — they need to confess as well that their fathers sinned." "<b>And this is</b> its saying ״וְהִתְוַדּוּ עֲוֹנָם וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶת עֲוֹן אֲבוֹתָם״ — in order to remove the error in which they stood, namely that this is not an evil deed, since the earlier ones did it and were not punished, and they would say with their mouth: ״Our fathers sinned and acted wickedly, and we have borne their iniquities״ (Lamentations 5:7), because they held the deeds of their fathers in their hands. And its saying <b>וְאַף אֲשֶׁר</b> etc. — meaning that the thought of this error, that the punishments were coming by chance (בְּקֶרִי), HaShem counts it as an iniquity that requires confession upon it like other transgressions."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The Talmudic citation (ברכות ז.) refers to Berakhot 7a; the abbreviation ז״ל is expanded throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:38.792649163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}